---
import "../styles/global.css";
import { SupportButton } from "../components/interactive/SupportButton";
import { reference } from "../data/reference";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#1ba11a" />
    <title>Save Pradnya Prameswara — A Retro Web Tribute</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="A playful early-2000s style tribute to Pradnya Prameswara, inspired by the fictional Save Walter White website from Breaking Bad. No donations are collected."
    />
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow" />
    <meta name="referrer" content="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="Save Pradnya Prameswara" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="Save Pradnya Prameswara — A Retro Web Tribute" />
    <meta property="og:description" content="A fictional old-school Breaking Bad-inspired tribute to a software developer. No real donations are collected." />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com" crossorigin />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net" crossorigin />
    <link rel="preload" as="image" href={reference.photo(1)} fetchpriority="high" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Comic+Neue:wght@400;700&display=swap" rel="stylesheet" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
  </head>
  <body style={`--reference-tile: url('${reference.tile}'); --reference-bow-left: url('${reference.bowLeft}'); --reference-bow-right: url('${reference.bowRight}')`}>
    <a class="skip-link" href="#main-content">Skip to content</a>
    <main id="main-content" class="page-wrap">
      <header class="site-heading">
        <h1>Pradnya Prameswara</h1>
        <p class="site-subtitle">Developer, Dreamer &amp; Problem Solver</p>
        <figure class="portrait hero-portrait">
          <img
            src={reference.photo(1)} data-fallback-src={reference.fallbackPhoto(1)}
            alt="Temporary reference photo of Walter White smiling, not a photo of Pradnya"
            width="402"
            height="517"
            fetchpriority="high"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </figure>
        <div class="photo-strip photo-strip-three" aria-label="Breaking Bad reference photo gallery">
          <figure class="portrait"><img src={reference.photo(2)} data-fallback-src={reference.fallbackPhoto(2)} width="247" height="322" alt="Temporary Breaking Bad reference photo of Walter White and his son" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure>
          <figure class="portrait"><img src={reference.photo(3)} data-fallback-src={reference.fallbackPhoto(3)} width="226" height="301" alt="Temporary Breaking Bad reference photo of Walter and a baby" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure>
          <figure class="portrait"><img src={reference.photo(4)} data-fallback-src={reference.fallbackPhoto(4)} width="220" height="343" alt="Temporary Breaking Bad reference photo of Walter with a family member" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure>
        </div>
        <figure class="portrait family-portrait">
          <img src={reference.photo(5)} data-fallback-src={reference.fallbackPhoto(5)} width="362" height="250" alt="Temporary Breaking Bad family reference photo" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </figure>
      </header>

      <section class="story-block intro-story" aria-label="Why save Pradnya">
        <p class="copy-yellow story-lead">
          My friend Pradnya is amazing. It is funny how easy it is to forget
          that until you see how many browser tabs, broken builds, and impossible
          deadlines one developer can face. Some people see a website. He sees
          a hundred things that could be made faster, cleaner, and easier to use.
          Sometimes it feels like the bugs are winning. But they are not.
          This is a page to remind him that there are people rooting for him!
        </p>
        <p class="copy-yellow">
          He spends a lot of time working on the tiny details that other people
          never notice. Buttons should work. Pages should fit on little phone
          screens. Forms should tell the truth. Even the smallest thing can turn
          into a really big problem if you ignore it for long enough. It would
          be easier to give up and call it finished. He usually opens another
          tab, looks at the problem again, and keeps going. So I think someone
          should make a ridiculous little website saying THANK YOU.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="photo-strip photo-strip-two" aria-label="More original reference photographs">
        <figure class="portrait"><img src={reference.photo(6)} data-fallback-src={reference.fallbackPhoto(6)} width="277" height="302" alt="Temporary photo of Walter White and Skyler from Breaking Bad" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure>
        <figure class="portrait"><img src={reference.photo(7)} data-fallback-src={reference.fallbackPhoto(7)} width="193" height="266" alt="Temporary photo portrait of Walter White from Breaking Bad" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure>
      </div>

      <section class="story-block long-story" aria-label="A message for Pradnya">
        <p>
          Pradnya works on websites and software. That probably sounds pretty
          normal until you sit next to a developer and see what actually happens.
          There are screens full of code, confusing messages that appear for no
          reason, and things that worked perfectly yesterday but have decided
          not to work today. It is a mystery to me how anybody understands it.
          He keeps explaining that there is a reason for every problem. I think
          he just likes solving puzzles that the rest of us cannot even read.
        </p>
        <p>
          He learned about technology and education at Universitas Pendidikan
          Ganesha in Bali. He likes working on the parts of a project that make
          life easier for other people, from the interface that you click on to
          the systems behind it. There are so many different things to think
          about. Sometimes a change that looks really small can affect an entire
          page. That is why he checks it on a phone, then a laptop, then another
          screen, and somehow always finds something else to improve.
        </p>
        <p>
          The thing is, a working website does not just happen. Somebody has to
          think about how it looks and how it behaves. Somebody has to make sure
          people can read it, find what they need, and use it without getting
          lost. And somebody has to fix the problems that show up after everyone
          else has gone home. He cares about those things. He wants the finished
          work to feel simple, even when building it was anything but simple.
        </p>
        <p>
          So if you are reading this and wondering why this page looks like it
          came straight out of the year 2009, there is a very good reason. It
          is a little tribute to the wonderfully strange internet we grew up
          with. It has huge letters, a crazy green background, photos that have
          nothing to do with the actual developer yet, and a dramatic button at
          the bottom. None of it is a real appeal for money. It is just a funny
          way of telling Pradnya to keep doing what he loves. The internet
          needs people who still care about making good things.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="appeal" aria-labelledby="appeal-title">
        <p class="appeal-line appeal-line-one">What a wonderful developer we have!</p>
        <p class="appeal-line appeal-line-two">But there are still bugs to fix. So many bugs!</p>
        <p id="appeal-title" class="appeal-line appeal-line-three">
          To help, please send your encouragement<br class="desktop-break" />
          and keep this developer in your thoughts!
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="action-area" aria-label="Support the project">
        <div class="retro-banner">
          <h2>Save Pradnya<br class="mobile-break" /> Prameswara</h2>
          <p>You can help keep the developer coding!</p>
        </div>
        <SupportButton client:load />
        <p class="no-payments">A parody tribute — no real donations or payments.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="counter-panel" aria-label="Decorative website counter">
        <p class="counter-headline">ANY AMOUNT OF SUPPORT WOULD BE APPRECIATED!</p>
        <p class="counter-subtitle">Visitors so far:</p>
        <div class="odometer" aria-label="Decorative counter, not actual visitor analytics">
          <span>0</span><span>0</span><span>0</span><span>0</span><span>0</span><span>1</span>
        </div>
        <p class="counter-notice">* Just an old-school decorative counter, not a live statistic.</p>
      </section>

      <footer class="site-footer">
        <nav aria-label="Footer navigation">
          <a href="https://github.com/PradnyaPrameswara" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">About &amp; Contact</a>
          <a href="#reference-note">About This Tribute</a>
          <a href="https://www.savewalterwhite.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Original Inspiration</a>
        </nav>
        <p id="reference-note" class="reference-note">
          Unofficial Breaking Bad-inspired fan homage. The seven photos are
          temporary Walter White reference images; they do not depict Pradnya.
          No affiliation with AMC or the series, and no donations are accepted.
        </p>
        <p class="copyright">© {new Date().getFullYear()} Save Pradnya Prameswara — just for fun.</p>
      </footer>
    </main>
    <script>import "../scripts/reference-images";</script>
  </body>
</html>
